WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.023
Human judgment is powerful, but it is not perfectly consistent.

2
00:00:04.023 --> 00:00:08.865
Cognitive biases are predictable tendencies that can influence reasoning.

3
00:00:08.865 --> 00:00:13.186
Noise is unwanted variation in judgments that should be consistent.

4
00:00:13.186 --> 00:00:15.347
Bias pushes judgment in a pattern.

5
00:00:15.347 --> 00:00:17.284
Noise makes judgment scatter.

6
00:00:17.284 --> 00:00:24.957
Both can damage decision quality, especially when teams assume that experience alone makes their conclusions reliable.

7
00:00:24.957 --> 00:00:30.693
Confirmation bias leads people to favor evidence that supports what they already believe.

8
00:00:30.693 --> 00:00:35.610
Anchoring makes the first number, idea, or proposal exert too much influence.

9
00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:40.378
Availability makes recent or vivid examples feel more common than they are.

10
00:00:40.378 --> 00:00:44.103
Overconfidence makes people underestimate uncertainty.

11
00:00:44.103 --> 00:00:51.478
Sunk cost makes it hard to stop investing in a weak path because time, money, or reputation has already been committed.

12
00:00:51.478 --> 00:00:54.011
Group settings create additional risk.

13
00:00:54.011 --> 00:00:59.375
Groupthink can make people suppress concerns to preserve harmony or avoid conflict.

14
00:00:59.375 --> 00:01:02.728
A senior person can unintentionally anchor the room.

15
00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:05.559
A team can confuse confidence with evidence.

16
00:01:05.559 --> 00:01:11.295
A fast-moving culture can reward decisive language while quietly punishing uncertainty.

17
00:01:11.295 --> 00:01:16.286
Decision hygiene is the set of habits that helps reduce these avoidable errors.

18
00:01:16.286 --> 00:01:26.642
Useful practices include checklists, explicit assumptions, independent estimates, pre-mortems, red-team review at a high level, and structured disagreement.

19
00:01:26.642 --> 00:01:32.974
A pre-mortem imagines that the decision failed in the future and asks what likely caused the failure.

20
00:01:32.974 --> 00:01:39.008
This makes it easier to surface risks before commitment, when the team can still change the plan.

21
00:01:39.008 --> 00:01:42.510
Decision hygiene does not assume perfect rationality.

22
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.427
It assumes people are busy, social, emotional, and operating under pressure.

23
00:01:47.427 --> 00:01:49.140
Structure helps judgment.

24
00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:56.143
It creates space for evidence, dissent, and review without requiring everyone to become detached or flawless.

25
00:01:56.143 --> 00:01:59.868
The point is not to remove human judgment, but to support it.
